# English Vocabulary and Usage Examples

## Common Words (A-Z)

### A
abandon (verb): to give up completely. The sailors had to abandon the sinking ship.
ability (noun): skill or power to do something. He has the ability to learn languages quickly.
able (adjective): having the means to do something. She is able to walk after the surgery.
about (preposition): on the subject of. We talked about the weather.
above (adverb): at a higher level or layer. The plane flew above the clouds.
abroad (adverb): in or to a foreign country. She wants to study abroad.
absence (noun): the state of being away. His absence was noticed immediately.
absolute (adjective): total; not limited. That is absolute nonsense.
absorb (verb): to take in or soak up. The sponge absorbed the water.
abstract (adjective): based on ideas rather than things. Freedom is an abstract concept.
abundant (adjective): existing in large quantities. The region has abundant wildlife.
accent (noun): distinct way of pronunciation. She speaks with a French accent.
accept (verb): to agree to receive something. I accepted the job offer.
access (noun): the means of approaching or entering. Students have access to the library.
accident (noun): an unexpected event causing harm. He was injured in a car accident.
accommodate (verb): to provide space or help. The hotel can accommodate 200 guests.
accompany (verb): to go somewhere with someone. She accompanied her friend to the party.
accomplish (verb): to achieve or complete successfully. We accomplished our mission.
account (noun): a record of financial transactions. I opened a bank account.
accurate (adjective): correct and true in every detail. The clock is accurate to the second.
achieve (verb): to successfully bring about. She achieved her dream of becoming a doctor.
acknowledge (verb): to accept or admit the existence of. He acknowledged his mistake.
acquire (verb): to get or gain possession of. She acquired a taste for classical music.
across (preposition): from one side to the other. They walked across the bridge.
act (verb): to take action; perform. You need to act now before it’s too late.
action (noun): the process of doing something. His quick action saved the child.
active (adjective): engaging in physical movement; lively. She stays active by cycling.
activity (noun): something that is done for pleasure or work. Painting is a relaxing activity.
actual (adjective): existing in fact; real. The actual cost was higher than estimated.
add (verb): to put together with something else. Add sugar to the tea.
addition (noun): the process of adding. In addition to her job, she volunteers.
address (noun): the location of a building. Please write your home address.
adequate (adjective): enough for a particular purpose. The supply was adequate for the trip.
adjust (verb): to alter slightly to fit. He adjusted his tie before the interview.
admire (verb): to regard with respect. I admire her determination.
admit (verb): to confess to be true. He admitted that he was wrong.
adopt (verb): to take up or start to use. The company adopts new technology quickly.
adult (noun): a fully grown person. The event is for adults only.
advance (verb): to move forward. The army advanced towards the city.
advantage (noun): a condition giving a greater chance of success. Height gave him an advantage.
adventure (noun): an exciting or unusual experience. Camping in the mountains was an adventure.
advertise (verb): to promote publicly. They advertise their products online.
advice (noun): guidance or recommendations. She gave me some good advice.
affair (noun): an event or matter. The wedding was a grand affair.
affect (verb): to have an influence on. The weather affected our travel plans.
afford (verb): to have enough money to pay for. I can’t afford a new car right now.
